[agent] ## Result
[agent] **Diverging feature: `f3`**
[agent] I did the comparison entirely on the platform via the `hops` CLI:
[agent] 1. Created two offline feature groups (`skew_train`, `skew_serve`) and ingested both CSVs (`hops fg insert --file`).
[agent] 2. Joined them on `entity_id` and computed per-feature mean absolute differences with `hops sql` (Trino, `delta` catalog).
[agent] **Findings:**
[agent] - `f1, f2, f4, f5` match exactly between training and serving (mean abs diff = 0).
[agent] - `f3` diverges (mean abs diff ≈ 8.19; ratio not constant, 1.55–15.07).
[agent] - The relationship is exact across all 200 shared entities: `training_f3 = log1p(serving_f3)` (i.e. `serving_f3 = exp(training_f3) − 1`), max residual ~6e-7.
[agent] **Cause:** The training pipeline applies a `log1p` transform to `f3` before storing it, but the online serving path serves the raw, untransformed value — classic missing-transform training/serving skew.
[agent] Note: the CLI's `hops sql` needed `--no-verify` (the Trino node's cert had an IP-SAN mismatch) and the FG offline tables live in the `delta` catalog under schema `mlpabd90f13_featurestore`, not the default `iceberg` catalog — worth knowing for anyone else using `hops sql` against this cluster.
